# Synchronicity tech stack

Prefer this stack. Do not swap core pieces without an explicit product decision.

## Locked choices

| Layer | Choice | Responsibility |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Monorepo | Turborepo | Packages / apps layout |
| Web + API | Next.js | Landing, pricing (later), dashboard, chat UI, API layer |
| ORM / DB | Prisma + Postgres | Users, sessions, billing, agent run / event logs |
| Cache / bus | Redis (Docker) | Session state / pub-sub |
| Jobs | Inngest | Spin up session, run agent, wait for human-in-loop, resume, cleanup |
| Realtime | WebSocket service (separate from Next.js) | Stream chat, live screenshots, user-control input |
| Browser | Playwright | Clicks, screenshots, navigation inside session container |
| Agent | OpenAI Agents SDK | Agent loop (screenshot -> decide -> act) + HITL pause / resume |
| Session isolation | Docker + Fly Machines / Fargate / Cloud Run Jobs | One isolated browser container per session |
| Media | Cloudinary | Store / serve screenshots |
| Errors | Sentry | Error tracking |
| Observability | Agents SDK tracing + Postgres event log | Debug / replay of agent runs |

## Architecture guardrails

- Keep WebSocket streaming **out of** the Next.js process (separate service)
- One browser container per session; never share browsers across users
- Inngest owns session lifecycle + HITL wait / resume; WebSocket owns live UI streams
- Persist agent run / events in Postgres for replay; use SDK tracing for debugging
